---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import NewsletterForm from "../../components/forms/newsletter-form.astro";
---
<Layout title="MKV Reference Guide" seo={{
  page_description: "Technical reference for the Matroska (MKV) container. Understand why it's the standard for high-quality archiving and how to play it on Apple devices.",
  open_graph_type: "article"
}}>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <div class="text-sm text-secondary mb-4">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-primary">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/reference/" class="hover:text-primary">Reference</a> &gt; MKV
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-8">MKV (Matroska)</h1>
    <div class="prose max-w-none mb-12">
      <p class="lead text-xl text-secondary">
        MKV is an open-standard, highly flexible <a href="/reference/containers">container format</a> widely used by enthusiasts for archiving high-quality movies, anime, and Blu-ray rips.
      </p>

      <h2>Why use MKV instead of MP4?</h2>
      <p>
        While <a href="/reference/mp4">MP4</a> is designed for broad compatibility, MKV is designed for maximum capability. An MP4 is generally restricted to a single video track and a few common audio tracks.
      </p>
      <p>An MKV file, on the other hand, can hold an unlimited number of video, audio, picture, and subtitle tracks in one file. A single MKV might contain:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A 4K <a href="/reference/h265">H.265</a> video stream.</li>
        <li>An English 5.1 Surround Sound track.</li>
        <li>A Japanese Stereo track.</li>
        <li>A Directors Commentary audio track.</li>
        <li>Five different subtitle languages in advanced SSA/ASS format.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Playback Problem</h2>
      <p>
        Because MKV is an open standard, major corporations like Apple historically refused to support it natively in their operating systems. If you try to open an MKV on a Mac using QuickTime, or sync it to an iPhone, it will fail.
      </p>
      <p>
        To play MKV files on macOS or Windows without converting them, you need a third-party media player like VLC or IINA. Alternatively, you can read our guide on <a href="/blog/play-mkv-mac">how to play MKV files on Mac</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Remuxing vs Transcoding</h2>
      <p>
        If you have an MKV file that you need to play on an Apple TV or upload to a website, you don't necessarily have to re-encode the video (which degrades quality). If the video inside the MKV is already <a href="/reference/h264">H.264</a>, you can simply "Remux" it—extracting the video stream from the MKV box and putting it into an MP4 box. This process takes seconds and preserves 100% of the quality.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can test converting MKV to MP4 using our <a href="/">local video converter</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-lg border border-border">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Stay Updated</h3>
      <p class="text-secondary mb-4">Get the latest video tips and format news.</p>
      <NewsletterForm form="newsletter" />
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
